---
export const prerender = true;
import Layout from '../../layouts/main.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../../components/CaseHeader.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import portfolio from '../../assets/projects/portfolio.jpg';
---

<Layout
  title="Personal Portfolio"
  description="The decisions behind this Astro portfolio: static content, small client-side scripts, responsive layouts and automated accessibility and interaction checks."
>
  <article class="wrap page">
    <CaseHeader
      title="Personal Portfolio"
      description="A place to explain what I work on and what I learn."
      stack="Astro / TypeScript / CSS / Playwright / Vercel"
    >
      <a class="text-link" href="https://freddiephilpot.dev">
        Visit website ↗
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pphilfre/astro-portfolio">
        Source ↗
      </a>
    </CaseHeader>
    <figure class="case-image">
      <a
        class="case-image-link"
        href={portfolio.src}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        aria-label="Open portfolio screenshot at full size (new tab)"
      >
        <Image
          loading="lazy"
          src={portfolio}
          alt="The portfolio home page with selected projects and links to my work"
          widths={[480, 800, 1440, 1920]}
          sizes="90vw"
          format="webp"
          quality={95}
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        The updated home-page layout, captured from the local site. Select the
        image to enlarge.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Why Astro</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          I wanted one place to bring together my applications, home lab and
          technical writing, with enough explanation to show what I have
          actually worked on. Most of that content is text and images, so
          Astro’s static rendering is a useful fit.
        </p>
        <p>
          Public content pages are prerendered into HTML. The contact endpoint
          runs on the server through the Vercel adapter, so the site can serve
          static case studies while still handling form submissions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Keeping it small</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Shared layouts and components keep project cards, headings, navigation
          and metadata consistent. Blog and CTF content use Astro collections,
          with drafts excluded from public routes and the sitemap.
        </p>
        <p>
          Client-side JavaScript is limited to interactions that use it:
          navigation, theme selection, project browsing, filters and the
          screenshot gallery. React hydrates the contact form when it becomes
          visible. Astro produces responsive WebP screenshots, and the variable
          font is hosted locally.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Checking the details</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          The project deck and mobile navigation need to work across narrow
          screens, short viewports and keyboard input. The gallery also needs to
          announce which screenshot is selected. Those behaviours are easy to
          miss when only reviewing a desktop screenshot.
        </p>
        <p>
          The browser checks exercise responsive layouts, overflow, image
          loading, keyboard controls, reduced motion and light/dark
          accessibility using Playwright and axe. Contact validation has
          separate unit tests; browser tests mock successful submission so they
          do not send real email.
        </p>
        <p>
          Astro check and the production build provide additional checks on the
          source and generated pages. Deployment uses the existing Vercel
          configuration, with cache policies for static assets and uncached
          contact responses.
        </p>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://github.com/pphilfre/astro-portfolio/blob/main/scripts/browser-check.mjs"
        >
          Browser checks ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Keeping motion outside the content</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          The focus gradient belongs to the field edge. The coloured layer sits
          behind an opaque input, inside an isolated stacking context, so it
          cannot cover the text or caret. This also avoids depending on mask
          compositing to cut out the centre of the gradient.
        </p>
        <p>
          A short reveal starts at the pointer position. After that, a CSS
          transform rotates the colour layer while the field has focus. Moving
          focus away removes that animation. Reduced-motion settings use a
          static border, and forced-colour mode uses the system focus outline.
        </p>
        <p>
          Stylesheets are emitted as fingerprinted files with a long-lived cache
          policy. Content update dates are stored explicitly rather than
          changing on every build. A fresh deployment therefore does not make an
          unchanged article appear recently reviewed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>What it has taught me</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          The challenge is keeping a small site understandable while adding more
          work to it. Reusing the existing components helps me improve the
          content without making each page behave differently.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am learning to treat accessibility, responsive behaviour and
          accurate writing as part of the implementation. A case study is more
          useful when it explains a real decision and its limits than when it
          simply lists technologies.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <a class="next-project" href="/projects/homelab">
      <span>Infrastructure case study</span>
      <strong>
        Home Lab <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </strong>
    </a>
  </article>
</Layout>
